-- title:
--   Unit-root rung at p over a level-Nr corner package
-- statement:
--   **Ambient data.** Let $\mathcal O$ be a complete discrete valuation domain of characteristic zero with finite residue field $k=\mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal O$ (completeness with respect to the maximal ideal), let $p$ be a prime with $p\neq 2$ whose image in $\mathcal O$ lies in the maximal ideal, and let $\bar\rho$ be a two-dimensional residual Galois representation over $k$ (a monoid homomorphism from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)=\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\,\mathbb Q\simeq_{\mathbb Q}\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\,\mathbb Q$ to $\mathrm{End}_k(V)$ with $\dim_k V=2$, trivial on the Galois group of some finite extension of $\mathbb Q$). It is assumed that $\bar\rho$ is absolutely irreducible (its base change to $\overline{k}$ has no proper non-zero stable subspace) and that, viewed through [`GaloisRepAdic.ofResidualGaloisRep`](def/GaloisRep_Adic.html#L196), it is ordinary at $p$: for every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $p$ in its non-units there is a line $L\subseteq V$ spanned by a basis vector, stable under the decomposition group of $P$, with $\bar\rho(\sigma)v-v\in L$ for all $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup and all $v\in V$.
--
--   **Level and ramification hypotheses.** $S$ is a finite set of primes with $p\in S$; $N$ is a non-zero natural number (also $Np\neq0$) with $p\nmid N$ and all prime divisors of $N$ in $S$. A subset $S_{\min}\subseteq S$ with $p\in S_{\min}$ is given such that, for primes $q\neq p$, $q\in S_{\min}$ if and only if $\bar\rho$ is ramified at $q$; every $q\in S_{\min}$ with $q\neq p$ divides $N$; every prime $q\neq p$ with $q\notin S_{\min}$ and $q\mid N$ satisfies $q^2\mid N$; no prime $q\neq p$ satisfies $q^3\mid N$; and for $q\in S_{\min}$, $q\neq p$, the representation is unipotent on inertia at $q$, i.e. the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ is $(X-1)^2$ for $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of any valuation subring over $q$.
--
--   **Auxiliary prime.** $r$ is a prime with $r\geq5$, $r\notin S$, $r\nmid Np$, $p\nmid r-1$, and (hypothesis `hrρ`) for every valuation subring $P$ over $r$ and every $\sigma$ that is a Frobenius element at $r$ for $P$, $\bigl(\operatorname{tr}\bar\rho(\sigma)\bigr)^2\neq (r+1)^2$ in $k$.
--
--   **Hecke data.** Weight-two cusp forms of levels $Np$ and $N$ are assumed to have an integral structure, i.e. the $\mathbb C$-span of the $\mathbb Z$-lattice of forms with integral $q$-expansion coefficients is everything. Ring homomorphisms $\theta_1:\mathbb T_{Np}\to k$ and $\theta_0:\mathbb T_N\to k$ are given on the anemic Hecke algebras [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) of weight $2$ and levels $Np$, $N$ away from $S$ (the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebras of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}$ of cusp forms generated by $T_\ell$ for $\ell$ prime, $\ell$ not dividing the level and $\ell\notin S$, and $U_q$ for primes $q$ dividing the level, $q\notin S$), such that (hypotheses `hθ₁`, `hθ₀`) for every prime $\ell\notin S$ not dividing the respective level and every Frobenius $\sigma$ at $\ell$ the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2-\theta_i(T_\ell)X+\ell$. Further, $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphisms $\pi_{T_0}:\,$`heckeLocal` $N\,S\,\mathcal O\,\theta_0\to\mathcal O$ and $\pi_{T_1}:\,$`heckeLocal` $(Np)\,S\,\mathcal O\,\theta_1\to\mathcal O$ are given on the localisations of the $\mathcal O$-Hecke algebras at the residual eigensystems, agreeing on the Hecke operators $T_\ell$ for all primes $\ell\notin S$ dividing neither $N$ nor $Np$ (hypothesis `hπ`), where [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.π`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L151) denotes the canonical maps from the anemic Hecke algebras.
--
--   **The pairing family.** $B_{\mathrm{fam}}$ assigns, to each modulus $M$, each subgroup $H\leq(\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ and each witness $h_1:\mathrm{LevelLE}\,M\,M\,\top\,H\,1$ (i.e. $M\mid M$, $1\mid M/M$ and the reduction condition), an $\mathcal O$-bilinear form with values in $\mathcal O$ on the submodule $W_{M,H}\subseteq H^1(M,H;\mathcal O)=\bigl(\mathrm{Additive}\,\Gamma_H(M)\to_+\mathcal O\bigr)$ obtained as the image under `iDegL` $M\,M\,\top\,H\,1$ of the parabolic homomorphisms on $\Gamma_\top(M)$ (those vanishing on all $\gamma$ with $\operatorname{tr}(\gamma)^2=4$). Hypothesis `hBfam` requires, for every $M\neq0$, $H$ and $h_1$ with $[(\mathbb Z/M)^\times:H]$ a unit in $\mathcal O$: that $B_{\mathrm{fam}}\,M\,H\,h_1$ be bijective onto the $\mathcal O$-dual of $W_{M,H}$ (perfectness); that it be Hecke self-adjoint in the sense that $B(Tx,y)=B(x,Ty)$ whenever $\ell\neq0$ is prime or divides $M$ and $Tx$, $Ty$ in $W_{M,H}$ have underlying classes `heckeT` $M\,H\,\ell\,\mathcal O$ applied to $x$, $y$; and that every $x\in W_{M,H}$ be fixed by all diamond operators `diamondL` $M\,H\,\mathcal O\,d$, $d\in(\mathbb Z/M)^\times$. Hypothesis `hBfam'` requires the degeneracy compatibility: given $M\mid M'$ (both non-zero), subgroups $H$, $H'$, witnesses $h_1$, $h_1'$ as above, degrees $d,d'\neq0$ with $\mathrm{LevelLE}\,M\,M'\,H\,H'\,d$ and $\mathrm{LevelLE}\,M\,M'\,H\,H'\,d'$, $dd'=M'/M$, $H'$ the exact preimage of $H$ under the units map, and both indices units in $\mathcal O$, then for $x\in W_{M,H}$, $y\in W_{M',H'}$, $ix\in W_{M',H'}$ with underlying class `iDegL` of degree $d$ applied to $x$, and $jy\in W_{M,H}$ with underlying class `jDegL` of degree $d'$ applied to $y$, one has $B_{\mathrm{fam}}\,M\,H\,h_1(jy,x)=B_{\mathrm{fam}}\,M'\,H'\,h_1'(y,ix)$.
--
--   **The given rung at level $Nr$.** $Nr\neq0$, and $H_0\leq(\mathbb Z/Nr)^\times$ consists exactly of those $v$ whose image in $\mathbb Z/r$ is $1$; $h_{10}:\mathrm{LevelLE}\,(Nr)\,(Nr)\,\top\,H_0\,1$. A commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra $\mathbb T_0$ acts on $H^1(Nr,H_0;\mathcal O)$ compatibly with $\mathcal O$, and $cd_0$ is a corner datum for it: an idempotent splitting of $\mathbb T_0$, an index $\mathrm{idx}$, and a level pairing on the corner ring $\mathbb T_0$-corner at $\mathrm{idx}$ and on the corner module, the latter being the image of the idempotent $e_{\mathrm{idx}}$ acting on $H^1(Nr,H_0;\mathcal O)$. An $\mathcal O$-algebra isomorphism $e_0$ identifies $cd_0$'s corner ring with `heckeLocal` $N\,S\,\mathcal O\,\theta_0$; the corner module is finite and free over $\mathcal O$; and $t_p$ is an element of the corner ring. The hypotheses on this rung are: `hT₀`, that for every prime $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid N$ and $\ell\nmid Nr$ the element $e_0^{-1}(\pi(T_\ell))$ acts on the corner module as `heckeT` at $\ell$; `htp`, that $t_p$ is a unit and acts on the corner module both as `heckeT` at $p$ and as `heckeTlower` at $p$; `hocc₀`, that the submodule of the corner module annihilated by $\ker(\pi_{T_0}\circ e_0)$ is non-zero; `hrk₀`, that $\operatorname{rank}_{\mathcal O}$ of the corner module equals the product of the $\mathcal O$-rank of that annihilated submodule with the $\mathcal O$-rank of the corner ring; `hgen`, that every $t\in\mathbb T_0$ acts on $H^1(Nr,H_0;\mathcal O)$ as some element of the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra generated by the range of [`CohCarrier.opFamily`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L91) $(Nr)\,H_0\,S\,\mathcal O$ (the operators attached to the generators `Gen`: $T_\ell$ for $\ell$ prime, $\ell\notin S$, $\ell\nmid Nr$; $U_q$ for primes $q\mid Nr$; and diamonds); `hfaith`, that the corner ring acts faithfully on the corner module; `hdia`, that every element of the corner submodule is fixed by all $\mathrm{diamondRaw}\,\sigma$, $\sigma\in\Gamma_0(Nr)$; `hne`, that there is a prime $\ell_0\nmid Nrp$ such that for every irreducible $\varpi\in\mathcal O$ and every $v$ in the corner module, $\mathrm{heckeT}\,\ell_0(v)-(\ell_0+1)v\in\varpi\cdot H^1(Nr,H_0;\mathcal O)$ implies $v=\varpi v_1$ for some $v_1$ in the corner module; `hW₀`, that the corner submodule is contained in the image under `iDegL` $(Nr)\,(Nr)\,\top\,H_0\,1$ of the parabolic homomorphisms; and `hP₀`, that $cd_0$'s pairing coincides on the corner module with $B_{\mathrm{fam}}\,(Nr)\,H_0\,h_{10}$ through that containment.
--
--   **Full-corner hypotheses.** A family $t_{\mathrm{full}}:\mathrm{Gen}\,(Nr)\,S\to\mathbb T_0$ and scalars $c_{\mathrm{full}}:\mathrm{Gen}\,(Nr)\,S\to\mathcal O$ are given; call a generator $g$ *admissible* if it is of the form $U_q$ only when $q\mid N$ (all other generators being admissible). Then: `htfull`, for admissible $g$, $t_{\mathrm{full}}(g)$ acts on $H^1(Nr,H_0;\mathcal O)$ as `opFamily`$(g)$; `hcfull`, for admissible $g$, $t_{\mathrm{full}}(g)-c_{\mathrm{full}}(g)$ lies in the ideal `cd₀.split.𝔪 cd₀.idx` attached to the splitting at the index; `hcofull`, a class $v\in H^1(Nr,H_0;\mathcal O)$ lies in the corner submodule whenever for every admissible $g$ and every $k$ there is $n$ with $\bigl(t_{\mathrm{full}}(g)-c_{\mathrm{full}}(g)\bigr)^n v\in\mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}^k\cdot H^1(Nr,H_0;\mathcal O)$; and `hcSigma`, that $c_{\mathrm{full}}(U_q)\in\mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}$ for every prime $q\mid Nr$ with $q\mid N$, $q\neq p$ and $q^2\mid N$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses $Nrp\neq0$ and there exist: a subgroup $H\leq(\mathbb Z/Nrp)^\times$, together with the identification of $H$ as the preimage of $H_0$ under the units map for $Nr\mid Nrp$; witnesses $h_{11}:\mathrm{LevelLE}\,(Nrp)\,(Nrp)\,\top\,H\,1$, $h_1:\mathrm{LevelLE}\,(Nr)\,(Nrp)\,H_0\,H\,1$ and $h_{p}:\mathrm{LevelLE}\,(Nr)\,(Nrp)\,H_0\,H\,p$; two commutative $\mathcal O$-algebras $\mathbb T_a$, $\mathbb T_1$ acting on $H^1(Nrp,H;\mathcal O)$ compatibly with $\mathcal O$; corner data $cd_a$ for $\mathbb T_a$ and $cd_1$ for $\mathbb T_1$ on $H^1(Nrp,H;\mathcal O)$; an $\mathcal O$-algebra isomorphism $e_1$ from $cd_1$'s corner ring onto `heckeLocal` $(Np)\,S\,\mathcal O\,\theta_1$; an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\mathrm{res}_1$ from $cd_1$'s corner ring to $cd_0$'s corner ring; finiteness and freeness over $\mathcal O$ of the corner modules of $cd_a$ and $cd_1$; and an element $\tilde\alpha$ of $cd_0$'s corner ring, such that all of the following hold.
--
--   (1) $\tilde\alpha$ is a unit, $\tilde\alpha^2-t_p\tilde\alpha+p=0$ in $cd_0$'s corner ring, and $\tilde\alpha-t_p$ lies in the ideal generated by the image of $\mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}$.
--
--   (2) $\mathrm{res}_1$ is surjective and $\pi_{T_1}\circ e_1=(\pi_{T_0}\circ e_0)\circ\mathrm{res}_1$.
--
--   (3) The corner submodule of $cd_1$ is contained in that of $cd_a$.
--
--   (4) There is an $\mathcal O$-submodule $C\subseteq H^1(Nrp,H;\mathcal O)$ such that the corner submodule of $cd_a$ consists exactly of the sums $v_1+v_2$ with $v_1$ in the corner submodule of $cd_1$ and $v_2\in C$; the corner submodule of $cd_1$ meets $C$ only in $0$; for $x,y$ in $cd_a$'s corner module with $x$ lying in $cd_1$'s corner submodule and $y$ in $C$, both $cd_a$-pairing values $B(x,y)$ and $B(y,x)$ vanish; and $C$ is stable under the action of $cd_a$'s corner ring on $cd_a$'s corner module.
--
--   (5) There is a surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\varphi$ from $cd_a$'s corner ring to $cd_1$'s corner ring compatible with the actions: whenever $x$ in $cd_a$'s corner module and $x_1$ in $cd_1$'s corner module have the same underlying class, the class underlying $t\cdot x$ equals that of $\varphi(t)\cdot x_1$; moreover for every prime $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid Np$ and $\ell\nmid Nrp$, the element $e_1^{-1}(\pi(T_\ell))$ acts on $cd_1$'s corner module as `heckeT` at $\ell$ and level $Nrp$.
--
--   (6) The pairings agree: if $x,y$ in $cd_1$'s corner module and $x',y'$ in $cd_a$'s corner module have the same underlying classes, then $cd_1$'s pairing on $(x,y)$ equals $cd_a$'s pairing on $(x',y')$.
--
--   (7) The submodule of $cd_1$'s corner module annihilated by $\ker(\pi_{T_1}\circ e_1)$ is non-zero, and $\operatorname{rank}_{\mathcal O}$ of $cd_1$'s corner module equals the product of the $\mathcal O$-rank of that annihilated submodule with the $\mathcal O$-rank of $cd_1$'s corner ring.
--
--   (8) Both degeneracy maps `iDegL` of degrees $1$ and $p$ from level $Nr$ to level $Nrp$ (indexed by $k\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$ through $![\,\cdot,\cdot\,]$) carry the corner submodule of $cd_0$ into the corner submodule of $cd_a$; and both trace maps `jDegL` of degrees $p$ and $1$ carry the corner submodule of $cd_a$ into the corner submodule of $cd_0$.
--
--   (9) For every $m$ in $cd_0$'s corner module, the class $\mathrm{iDegL}_1(\tilde\alpha\cdot m)-\mathrm{iDegL}_p(m)$ lies in the corner submodule of $cd_1$.
--
--   (10) $\mathrm{res}_1$ intertwines the actions along both traces: for $t_1$ in $cd_1$'s corner ring, $m'$ in $cd_1$'s corner module and elements $jm$, $jtm$ of $cd_0$'s corner module whose underlying classes are $\mathrm{jDegL}_p(m')$ and $\mathrm{jDegL}_p(t_1\cdot m')$ respectively, one has $jtm=\mathrm{res}_1(t_1)\cdot jm$; and the same statement with $\mathrm{jDegL}_1$ in place of $\mathrm{jDegL}_p$.
--
--   (11) Saturation: if $a\in\mathcal O$ is non-zero, $m'$ is in $cd_1$'s corner module and $a\,m'$ lies in $\ker(\pi_{T_1}\circ e_1)\cdot(cd_1\text{'s corner module})$, then $m'$ lies there too; and the $\mathcal O$-ranks of the submodules annihilated by $\ker(\pi_{T_1}\circ e_1)$ and by $\ker(\pi_{T_0}\circ e_0)$, in the corner modules of $cd_1$ and $cd_0$ respectively, are equal.
--
--   (12) The corner submodule of $cd_a$ is contained in the image under `iDegL` $(Nrp)\,(Nrp)\,\top\,H\,1$ of the parabolic homomorphisms on $\Gamma_\top(Nrp)$, and through this containment $cd_a$'s pairing coincides with $B_{\mathrm{fam}}\,(Nrp)\,H\,h_{11}$.
--
--   (13) The two legs are adjoint for the pairings: for $m'$ in $cd_a$'s corner module, $m$, $jm$ in $cd_0$'s corner module and $im$ in $cd_a$'s corner module with $jm$ underlain by $\mathrm{jDegL}_p(m')$ and $im$ by $\mathrm{iDegL}_1(m)$, one has $cd_0$-pairing$(jm,m)=cd_a$-pairing$(m',im)$; and likewise with the degrees exchanged, $jm$ underlain by $\mathrm{jDegL}_1(m')$ and $im$ by $\mathrm{iDegL}_p(m)$.
--
--   This is the inductive step that passes from a corner package of Hecke modules at level $Nr$ to one at level $Nrp$ in the ordinary situation, producing the anemic corner datum $cd_a$ at the higher level, its unit-root refinement $cd_1$ with corner ring the localised Hecke algebra of level $Np$, the unit root $\tilde\alpha$ of $X^2-t_pX+p$, and the compatibility of the degeneracy and trace maps with the level pairings. It is used by [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.exists_heckeModule_rung_at_residueChar_unitRoot_of_cornerData_of_fullCorner_of_not_cube_dvd`](thm.html#CuspForm.heckeLocal.exists_heckeModule_rung_at_residueChar_unitRoot_of_cornerData_of_fullCorner_of_not_cube_dvd), which assembles the rung at the residue characteristic in the Hecke-module input to modularity lifting.
